 "result": "{\"answer\": \"The European Union has 24 official languages. They are: Bulgarian, Croatian, Czech, Danish, Dutch, English, Estonian, Finnish, French, German, Greek, Hungarian, Irish, Italian, Latvian, Lithuanian, Maltese, Polish, Portuguese, Romanian, Slovak, Slovenian, Spanish and Swedish.\\n\\nA few notes: the number has stood at 24 since Croatian was added when Croatia joined in July 2013. English remains an official language even after Brexit, because it is also an official language of Ireland and Malta (though Ireland nominated Irish and Malta nominated Maltese). Irish had a derogation limiting the amount of material translated into it, but that was fully phased out on 1 January 2022, so it now has full official-language status. Spain has requested official status for Catalan, Basque and Galician, but as of my knowledge that has not been approved (it requires unanimity in the Council).\", \"confidence\": \"high\", \"abstained\": false}",
